### Configure word token in Tree-sitter grammar

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/blob/master/docs/src/creating-parsers/3-writing-the-grammar.md

Demonstrates how to define a 'word' token within a JavaScript grammar definition. By specifying an identifier rule as the word token, Tree-sitter automatically treats matching strings as keywords, enhancing lexer efficiency.

```javascript
grammar({
  name: "javascript",

  word: $ => $.identifier,

  rules: {
    expression: $ =>
      choice(
        $.identifier,
        $.unary_expression,
        $.binary_expression,
        // ...
      ),

    binary_expression: $ =>
      choice(
        prec.left(1, seq($.expression, "instanceof", $.expression)),
        // ...
      ),

    unary_expression: $ =>
      choice(
        prec.left(2, seq("typeof", $.expression)),
        // ...
      ),

    identifier: $ => /[a-z_]+/,
  },
});
```

### Using Extras

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter/blob/master/docs/src/creating-parsers/3-writing-the-grammar.md

Extras are tokens that can appear anywhere in the grammar without being explicitly mentioned in every rule, which is ideal for elements like whitespace and comments. To define an extra, use the extras function in your grammar configuration. For complex tokens, it is best practice to define them as a rule and reference that rule in the extras array rather than inlining the pattern. This strategy prevents the lexer from inlining the pattern repeatedly, which significantly reduces the overall parser size. Additionally, Tree-sitter optimizes the whitespace character class, \s, to [ \t\n\r] for performance reasons, as most language implementations do not require the full Unicode definition of whitespace.

